        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 501,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 523,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 502,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73326,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 502,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histiocytic sarcoma can involve any site of the CNS and the meninges.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 524,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 503,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73327,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 503,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Neurological symptoms are related to tumour location. Neuroimaging mimics a malignant primary CNS tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 525,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 504,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73328,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 504,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fewer than 100&nbsp;cases have been reported, most of them in adults {30185195}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 526,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 505,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73329,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 505,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Isolated cases of radiation-associated histiocytic sarcoma of the CNS have been reported {22997353; 23356953}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 527,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 506,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73330,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 506,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A study of 28&nbsp;histiocytic sarcomas found RAS/MAPK pathway gene alterations in 16&nbsp;cases (57%; affecting mostly <em>MAP2K1</em>, <em>KRAS</em>, and <em>BRAF</em>, but also<em> NRAS</em>, <em>PTPN11</em>, <em>NF1</em>, and <em>CBL</em>) and PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway gene alterations in 6&nbsp;cases (21%; in <em>PTEN</em>, <em>MTOR</em>, <em>PIK3R1</em>, and <em>PIK3CA</em>) {30626916}. Alterations in both pathways were not mutually exclusive. In addition, <em>CDKN2A</em> and/or <em>CDKN2B</em> was altered in 13&nbsp;cases (46%), half of which displayed homozygous deletions. Independently, a recent study reported single cases with <em>KRAS</em>, <em>NRAS</em>, or <em>CSF1R</em> mutations, or <em>BRAF</em> fusion {31768065}. A single case is described with concurrent <em>BRAF</em> p.F595L and <em>HRAS</em> mutation {26582644}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 534,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 513,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73331,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 513,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histiocytic sarcomas are destructive, soft, fleshy, white masses with occasional yellow necrotic foci.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 528,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 507,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73332,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 507,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histiocytic sarcoma is characterized by highly cellular, non-cohesive infiltrates of large, moderately pleomorphic, mitotically active histiocytes, which have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, variably indented to irregular nuclei, and often prominent nucleoli. Occasional multinucleated or spindled forms are also common, as is background reactive inflammation {24807104; 11684953}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n<p>The tumour cells are typically positive for histiocytic markers (e.g. CD68, CD163, lysozyme, CD11c, and CD14); variably positive for CD34; and negative for myeloid antigens, dendritic antigens, CD30, ALK, and other lymphoid markers, as well as for glial, epithelial, and melanocytic antigens. The tumour cells are negative for the follicular dendritic cell antigens CD23 and CD35. However, follicular dendritic cell sarcoma expressing these antigens may primarily arise in the brain and must be differentiated from histiocytic sarcoma {14705740}. Although MAPK alterations are common, BRAF p.V600E expression is rare {28748542; 30626916}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 529,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 508,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73333,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 508,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 530,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 509,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73334,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 178,
        "headingId": 509,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although the proportion of <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E&ndash;mutant cases is relatively small, a variety of other MAPK gene alterations have been reported in non-CNS histiocytic sarcoma, so high-throughput sequencing may be advisable. Sequencing should cover all the potentially affected genes of the MAPK pathway (<em>MAP2K1</em>, <em>KRAS</em>, <em>NRAS</em>, <em>PTPN11</em>, <em>NF1</em>, and <em>CBL</em>), the reported altered genes of the mTOR pathway (<em>PTEN</em>, <em>MTOR</em>, <em>PIK3R1</em>, and <em>PIK3CA</em>), and<em> CSF1R</em> mutations. Rare <em>BRAF</em> fusions can be detected via in&nbsp;situ hybridization or RNA sequencing. <em>CDKN2A</em> and/or <em>CDKN2B</em> alterations often constitute homozygous deletions, which can be detected by in&nbsp;situ hybridization or array-based approaches.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:20:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
